# the argument of aristotle-politics-3.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Only one main conclusion is allowed; I took S5's verdict on the commendation of man and citizen.
# S2's "may be the same" is a granted supposition, not a modal claim, so it is written as a plain premise.
atom	man_eq_magistrate	the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same
atom	citizen_obeys	a citizen is one who obeys the magistrate
atom	magistrate_diff_citizen	the virtue of the magistrate is different from the virtue of the citizen
atom	man_eq_citizen	the virtue of the good man is in general the same as the virtue of the citizen
atom	citizen_both	the virtue of a good citizen is to know how to command as well as to obey, and to do both well
atom	man_only_command	the virtue of a good man consists only in being able to command
atom	same_commendation	the commendation of the good man and of the good citizen is the same
P1	S2	man_eq_magistrate
P2	S2	citizen_obeys
P3	S2	magistrate_diff_citizen
P4	S2	man_eq_magistrate ∧ citizen_obeys ∧ magistrate_diff_citizen → ¬man_eq_citizen
C1	S2	¬man_eq_citizen	from P1 P2 P3 P4
P5	S4	citizen_both	kind normative
P6	S5	man_only_command ∧ citizen_both → ¬same_commendation
# The text plainly takes the good man's virtue to be the magistrate's, that is, ruling.
A1	-	man_eq_magistrate → man_only_command
C2	S5	man_only_command	from P1 A1
C	S5	¬same_commendation	from C2 P5 P6
